1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,700
Path loss is the biggest number in radio.

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:10,101
It's the attenuation a signal suffers just travelling from transmitter to receiver

3
00:00:10,101 --> 00:00:15,106
— dominated by the spreading of energy over distance, plus whatever terrain,

4
00:00:15,106 --> 00:00:20,309
buildings, and foliage take on top — and it's the single largest term in almost

5
00:00:20,309 --> 00:00:21,495
every link budget.

6
00:00:21,495 --> 00:00:23,141
Cables cost you a few dB.

7
00:00:23,141 --> 00:00:24,590
Connectors, fractions.

8
00:00:24,590 --> 00:00:25,183
The path?

9
00:00:25,183 --> 00:00:28,410
It can exceed a hundred dB over a few kilometres.

10
00:00:28,860 --> 00:00:32,145
Here's the physical picture, and it's worth owning.

11
00:00:32,145 --> 00:00:37,299
In empty space, a transmitter radiates its power over an expanding sphere — like

12
00:00:37,299 --> 00:00:42,452
paint on an inflating balloon, the same energy stretched over ever more surface.

13
00:00:42,452 --> 00:00:47,670
Your antenna is a fixed-size patch on that sphere, so the power crossing it falls

14
00:00:47,670 --> 00:00:51,148
as the square of the distance: the inverse-square law.

15
00:00:51,148 --> 00:00:53,467
Double the range, quarter the power.

16
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:58,556
In decibel terms, free-space loss rises twenty dB for every tenfold increase in

17
00:00:58,556 --> 00:01:03,903
range — and, for a fixed-size antenna, another twenty dB for every tenfold increase

18
00:01:03,903 --> 00:01:04,741
in frequency.

19
00:01:04,741 --> 00:01:09,701
The Friis transmission equation wraps this idealised free-space case into one

20
00:01:09,701 --> 00:01:10,216
formula.

21
00:01:10,666 --> 00:01:12,826
But you don't live in empty space.

22
00:01:12,826 --> 00:01:17,780
The catch-all model writes received power as falling with distance raised to a

23
00:01:17,780 --> 00:01:19,177
path-loss exponent, n.

24
00:01:19,177 --> 00:01:20,892
Free space is n equals two.

25
00:01:20,892 --> 00:01:25,656
Cluttered urban and indoor settings run two-point-seven to four — and heavy

26
00:01:25,656 --> 00:01:27,879
obstruction pushes it higher still.

27
00:01:27,879 --> 00:01:32,706
On top of that trend sit two extra effects: shadowing, the slow variation as

28
00:01:32,706 --> 00:01:37,914
terrain and buildings block the path, typically modelled as a log-normal spread of

29
00:01:37,914 --> 00:01:42,995
a few dB — and multipath fading, the fast fluctuation as reflected copies of the

30
00:01:42,995 --> 00:01:44,710
signal arrive out of phase.

31
00:01:44,710 --> 00:01:48,458
A link budget reserves a fade margin to survive those dips.

32
00:01:48,908 --> 00:01:51,629
And not all of the loss is bulk absorption.

33
00:01:51,629 --> 00:01:56,756
Diffraction over rooftops and hills, reflection off the ground and buildings, and

34
00:01:56,756 --> 00:02:01,946
blockage of the Fresnel zone — the region around the direct line the wave actually

35
00:02:01,946 --> 00:02:04,605
needs — all shape how much energy arrives.

36
00:02:04,605 --> 00:02:09,479
Which leads to the most cost-effective fact in this whole segment: raising an

37
00:02:09,479 --> 00:02:14,542
antenna even a few metres, clearing obstacles and opening that Fresnel zone, can

38
00:02:14,542 --> 00:02:17,644
buy more than a large increase in transmit power.

39
00:02:17,644 --> 00:02:19,100
Geometry beats wattage.

40
00:02:19,550 --> 00:02:24,688
Because the path so often totals a hundred dB or more, it dwarfs the handful of dB

41
00:02:24,688 --> 00:02:29,826
you can claw back with better cable or a preamp — the big wins are height, a clear

42
00:02:29,826 --> 00:02:32,772
line of sight, and a band that suits the range.

43
00:02:32,772 --> 00:02:37,973
Lower frequencies generally carry farther for the same power — less free-space loss

44
00:02:37,973 --> 00:02:42,860
and better diffraction around obstacles — one reason VHF public-safety systems

45
00:02:42,860 --> 00:02:44,866
reach farther per site than UHF.

46
00:02:44,866 --> 00:02:49,879
And path loss explains the shape of coverage: signal strength doesn't fall off a

47
00:02:49,879 --> 00:02:54,766
cliff at some range, it decays smoothly with the logarithm of distance, fading

48
00:02:54,766 --> 00:02:58,526
gradually into the noise floor rather than stopping sharply.

49
00:02:58,976 --> 00:03:04,067
On your own waterfall, path loss is the whole story of that faint, distant system

50
00:03:04,067 --> 00:03:08,090
sitting barely above the noise with hardly enough SNR to decode.

51
00:03:08,090 --> 00:03:13,055
GopherTrunk receives whatever the path delivers — so when a known nearby system

52
00:03:13,055 --> 00:03:18,272
won't decode, path loss is usually the first suspect: an obstruction, a low antenna

53
00:03:18,272 --> 00:03:20,974
— checked before anything in the DSP chain.

54
00:03:21,424 --> 00:03:26,839
So: path loss is the expanding sphere doing its arithmetic — twenty dB per decade

55
00:03:26,839 --> 00:03:31,986
in free space, worse in clutter, softened or saved by geometry — and it's why

56
00:03:31,986 --> 00:03:35,863
antenna height is the cheapest hundred dB fix that exists.

57
00:03:35,863 --> 00:03:37,668
Full write-up linked below.

